// batch_norm1d_test.mbt — ASP's BatchNorm1d behind three gates.
//
// WHY THREE GATES AND NOT ONE.
//
// The obvious gate for a normalisation layer is "analytic gradient vs central
// finite differences". Measured on this very layer, that gate catches 2 of 6
// plausible implementation mistakes. The other four pass with zero
// coordinates out of tolerance while producing wrong numbers, because a finite
// difference differentiates the very forward it is checking: if the forward
// drops the epsilon floor, the difference faithfully reports the gradient OF
// THE WRONG FUNCTION. Self-consistency is not correctness.
//
// So the gates are split by what each one can actually see:
//
//   G1  forward vs PyTorch goldens        -- sees forward semantics
//   G2  analytic gradient vs PyTorch      -- sees the backward
//       autograd goldens
//   G3  mutation sweep                    -- measures G1 and G2, and asserts
//                                            that G2's weaker cousin (FD)
//                                            is provably insufficient
//
// G3 is the one that matters long-term. It fails if someone ever replaces the
// PyTorch golden with a finite difference, which is the specific edit that
// would turn these gates back into the thing they exist to be better than.
//
// The goldens live in `batch_norm1d_golden.mbt`, generated by
// `verify/gen_bn1d_golden.py` from torch 2.10.0+cu130.

///|
///|```text
///|BN1D_TOL_FWD : relative term of the forward gate's tolerance
///|```

///|
///|```text
///|BN1D_TOL_FWD
///|```

const BN1D_TOL_FWD : Float = 1.0e-5F

///|
///|```text
///|BN1D_ATOL_GRAD / BN1D_TOL_GRAD : gradient gate's bound
///|```

///|
///|```text
///|BN1D_ATOL_GRAD
///|```

///| `d_x` on this fixture spans 6.1e-03 to 2.47e+02 -- a factor of 40156 --
///| because the tightest channel is normalised by an `inv_std` of 54 while the
///| loosest is normalised by 1.4. A pure relative test on such a tensor is not
///| measuring accuracy, it is measuring how close each coordinate happens to
///| sit to zero.
///
///| The absolute term is 2.5x the measured float32 residue, which
///| `verify/bn1d_backward_probe.py` attributes by decomposing
///| `d_input = dxhat*inv + d_var*2*xc/nhw + d_mean/nhw` term by term: the
///| error is dominated not by the backward's own arithmetic but by the
///| FORWARD's error propagating onward, since `dy = 2*w*y` inherits `y`'s own
///| 8.8e-05 and is then multiplied by `inv` = 54. Measured worst absolute
///| error 1.97e-02.
///
///| A symbolic bound from the largest term, `2*(FLT_EPS/2)*1323.7*sqrt(28)`,
///| comes to 8.4e-04 and is 24x too tight -- which is the useful part of that
///| measurement: it says the residue is inherited, not locally generated, so
///| it has to be bounded from measurement rather than from the visible terms.
///|
const BN1D_ATOL_GRAD : Float = 5.0e-2F

///|
///|```text
///|BN1D_TOL_GRAD : relative term of the gradient gate's tolerance
///|```

///|
///|```text
///|BN1D_TOL_GRAD
///|```

///| One percent. Every logic error these gates exist to catch -- a dropped
///| coupling term, a transposed sign, a missing `inv_std` -- moves a gradient
///| by 15% or more on this fixture, and the mutation sweep below reports the
///| measured figure for each. The float32 residue is 0.35% at worst, so this
///| sits an order of magnitude clear of both.
///|
const BN1D_TOL_GRAD : Float = 1.0e-2F

///|
///|```text
///|BN1D_FD_TOL : tolerance of the finite-difference column in G3
///|```

///|
///|```text
///|BN1D_FD_TOL
///|```

///| This one is NOT chosen to be tight. It is set by what the instrument can
///| actually resolve.
///
///| The gate loss is ~394, so its float32 ulp is ~3e-05, and it is a sum of 168
///| terms; differentiating it by central differences in float32 therefore
///| carries an absolute noise floor of order 0.1. Measured on the CORRECT
///| implementation, the worst error of a central difference is 1.3e-01 at the
///| best step size (h = 1e-02), 1.5e+00 at h = 1e-03 and 2.4e+01 at h = 1e-04
///| (`verify/bn1d_fd_probe.py`). This value sits just above that floor.
///|
///| The consequence is the point of the whole file: a finite-difference gate at
///| any usable tolerance cannot see a forward error of a few percent, so it
///| cannot be the thing that checks this layer.
///|
const BN1D_FD_TOL : Float = 5.0e-1F

///|
///|```text
///|count_over : coordinates outside a mixed absolute/relative bound
///|```

///|
///|```text
///|count_over
///|```

///| The bound is `|got - want| > atol + rtol * |want|`, not a pure relative one.
///
///| This is not a loosened tolerance; it is the correct scale, and the fixture
///| is what says so. The forward is `gamma * (x - mean) * inv_std + beta`, a
///| difference of two float32 numbers of magnitude ~7.5 scaled by an `inv_std`
///| as large as 54. Measured against this fixture the worst ABSOLUTE error is
///| flat at 8.8e-5 across all 168 coordinates, while the worst RELATIVE error
///| swings over two decades purely as a function of how near that particular
///| coordinate sits to zero. Judging such a quantity by its own relative size
///| measures how close it is to zero rather than how well it was computed.
///
///| `atol` is derived from the arithmetic in `gen_bn1d_golden.py`, not chosen
///| to make this pass: it is `2 * FLT_EPS/2 * max|x| * max(inv_std) * sqrt(n*w)`.
///|
fn count_over(
  got : Array[Float],
  want : Array[Float],
  atol : Float,
  rtol : Float,
) -> (Int, Float) {
  let mut over = 0
  let mut worst = 0.0F
  for i in 0..<want.length() {
    let diff = (got[i] - want[i]).abs()
    let bound = atol + rtol * want[i].abs()
    let rel = diff / bound
    if diff > bound { over = over + 1 }
    if rel > worst { worst = rel }
  }
  (over, worst)
}

///|
///|```text
///|d_loss_d_y : gradient of the gate's loss w.r.t. the BN output
///|```

///|
///|```text
///|d_loss_d_y
///|```

///| The loss is `SUM_n w_n * SUM_{c,w} y[n,c,w]^2`, so `dL/dy = w_n * 2 * y`.
///| The batch weights `w_n` are deliberately NOT all equal; see the fixture
///| note in `gen_bn1d_golden.py` for what a uniform weight would hide.
///|
fn d_loss_d_y(y : Array[Float]) -> Array[Float] {
  let n = BN1D_N
  let c = BN1D_C
  let w = BN1D_W
  let cw = c * w
  let out : Array[Float] = Array::make(y.length(), 0.0F)
  for ni in 0..<n {
    let wt = bn1d_loss_weights[ni]
    for k in 0..<cw {
      out[ni * cw + k] = wt * 2.0F * y[ni * cw + k]
    }
  }
  out
}

///|
///|```text
///|bn1d_fixture : a BatchNorm2d carrying the golden affine and eps
///|```

///|
///|```text
///|bn1d_fixture
///|```

fn bn1d_fixture() -> @nn.BatchNorm2d {
  @nn.BatchNorm2d::with_gamma_beta(
    bn1d_gamma.copy(),
    bn1d_beta.copy(),
    momentum = 0.0,
    eps = BN1D_EPS,
  )
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- G1

///|
///|```text
///|G1: ASP BatchNorm1d forward equals torch.nn.BatchNorm1d
///|```

///|
///|```text
///|G1 forward vs PyTorch
///|```

test "G1 / BatchNorm1d forward matches torch.nn.BatchNorm1d at ASP layout" {
  let bn = bn1d_fixture()
  let (y, _) = @nn.batch_norm1d_forward(
    bn1d_input.copy(),
    BN1D_N,
    BN1D_C,
    BN1D_W,
    bn,
  )
  let (over, worst) = count_over(y, bn1d_y_golden, BN1D_FWD_ATOL, BN1D_TOL_FWD)
  if over > 0 {
    fail(
      "G1: \{over}/\{bn1d_y_golden.length()} forward coordinates outside " +
      "tolerance, worst relative \{worst}",
    )
  }
}

///|
///|```text
///|G1a: the per-channel statistics the forward used are the batch ones
///|```

///| Pins the reduction the gate depends on. If a future change reduced over
///| the time axis alone, `inv_std` would still look plausible per channel while
///| every sample in the batch stopped contributing -- and G2's `d_x` would go
///| quiet in exactly the same way.
///|
test "G1a / per-channel mean and variance are the batch statistics" {
  let bn = bn1d_fixture()
  let (_, cache) = @nn.batch_norm1d_forward(
    bn1d_input.copy(),
    BN1D_N,
    BN1D_C,
    BN1D_W,
    bn,
  )
  if cache.h != 1 {
    fail("G1a: cache h = \{cache.h}, expected 1")
  }
  if cache.nhw != BN1D_N * BN1D_W {
    fail(
      "G1a: cache nhw = \{cache.nhw}, expected n*w = \{BN1D_N * BN1D_W}. " +
      "A different divisor means the reduction does not span the batch.",
    )
  }
  if !cache.coupled {
    fail("G1a: cache reports the statistics as decoupled (inference mode)")
  }
  let mut worst_mean = 0.0F
  let mut worst_inv = 0.0F
  for ci in 0..<BN1D_C {
    let dm = (cache.mean[ci] - bn1d_mean[ci]).abs() / bn1d_mean[ci].abs()
    worst_mean = if dm > worst_mean { dm } else { worst_mean }
    let want_inv = 1.0F / @core.sqrtf(bn1d_var[ci] + BN1D_EPS)
    let di = (cache.inv_std[ci] - want_inv).abs() / want_inv
    worst_inv = if di > worst_inv { di } else { worst_inv }
  }
  if worst_mean > 1.0e-5F {
    fail("G1a: worst relative per-channel mean error \{worst_mean}")
  }
  if worst_inv > 1.0e-5F {
    fail("G1a: worst relative inv_std error \{worst_inv}")
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- G2

///|
///|```text
///|G2: analytic gradients equal torch autograd
///|```

///|
///|```text
///|G2 gradients vs PyTorch autograd
///|```

test "G2 / d_x, d_gamma, d_beta match torch autograd" {
  let bn = bn1d_fixture()
  let (y, cache) = @nn.batch_norm1d_forward(
    bn1d_input.copy(),
    BN1D_N,
    BN1D_C,
    BN1D_W,
    bn,
  )
  let dy = d_loss_d_y(y)
  let (dx, dg, db) = @nn.batch_norm1d_backward(dy, cache, bn)
  let (ox, wx) = count_over(dx, bn1d_dx_golden, BN1D_ATOL_GRAD, BN1D_TOL_GRAD)
  if ox > 0 {
    fail(
      "G2: d_x has \{ox}/\{bn1d_dx_golden.length()} coordinates outside " +
      "tolerance, worst relative \{wx}",
    )
  }
  let (og, wg) = count_over(dg, bn1d_dgamma_golden, BN1D_ATOL_GRAD, BN1D_TOL_GRAD)
  if og > 0 {
    fail("G2: d_gamma has \{og}/\{BN1D_C} outside tolerance, worst \{wg}")
  }
  let (ob, wb) = count_over(db, bn1d_dbeta_golden, BN1D_ATOL_GRAD, BN1D_TOL_GRAD)
  if ob > 0 {
    fail("G2: d_beta has \{ob}/\{BN1D_C} outside tolerance, worst \{wb}")
  }
}

///|
///|```text
///|G2a: d_input is genuinely cross-sample coupled
///|```

///| The reason BatchNorm1d at `h = 1` exists as its own concept: the
///| statistics span the batch, so `d_x[n]` must depend on `x[m]` for `m != n`.
///| If a backward ever dropped the two coupling terms it would still be
///| self-consistent, and G2 above would still pass, because G2's fixture also
///| moves the other samples. So this test asserts the COUPLING is present,
///| independently of the gradient's value.
///|
test "G2a / d_input depends on the other samples in the batch" {
  let bn = bn1d_fixture()
  let (y, cache) = @nn.batch_norm1d_forward(
    bn1d_input.copy(),
    BN1D_N,
    BN1D_C,
    BN1D_W,
    bn,
  )
  let dy = d_loss_d_y(y)
  let (dx, _, _) = @nn.batch_norm1d_backward(dy, cache, bn)

  // Zero d_output for every sample but the last, then ask whether the last
  // sample's d_x moves. It must: the coupling terms are sums over the batch.
  let masked : Array[Float] = Array::make(dy.length(), 0.0F)
  let last = (BN1D_N - 1) * BN1D_C * BN1D_W
  for k in 0..<(BN1D_C * BN1D_W) {
    masked[last + k] = dy[last + k]
  }
  let (dx_masked, _, _) = @nn.batch_norm1d_backward(masked, cache, bn)

  // With the loss concentrated on one sample, a per-sample (uncoupled)
  // backward would leave that sample's own gradient identical and put nothing
  // anywhere. Sum the other samples' contributions.
  let mut moved = 0.0F
  for k in 0..<last {
    moved = moved + (dx_masked[k] - dx[k]).abs()
  }
  if moved <= 0.0F {
    fail(
      "G2a: perturbing one sample's d_output left every other sample's d_x " +
      "unchanged, so d_input is per-sample and the batch statistics are not " +
      "being differentiated",
    )
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- G3

///|
///|```text
///|BnMutant : the implementation mistakes G3 measures the gates against
///|```

///|
///|```text
///|BnMutant
///|```

pub(all) enum BnMutant {
  Correct
  NoEps
  NoMeanSub
  NoSqrt
  NoGamma
  NoBeta
  SwapAB
  PerSample
  Unbiased
}

///|
///|```text
///|same_mutant
///|```

///|
///|```text
///|same_mutant
///|```

///| A structural comparison rather than `==`, so the enum needs no `Eq` impl
///| and no derived methods are promoted implicitly.
///|
fn same_mutant(a : BnMutant, b : BnMutant) -> Bool {
  match (a, b) {
    (Correct, Correct) => true
    (NoEps, NoEps) => true
    (NoMeanSub, NoMeanSub) => true
    (NoSqrt, NoSqrt) => true
    (NoGamma, NoGamma) => true
    (NoBeta, NoBeta) => true
    (SwapAB, SwapAB) => true
    (PerSample, PerSample) => true
    (Unbiased, Unbiased) => true
    _ => false
  }
}

///|
///|```text
///|mutant_name
///|```

///|
///|```text
///|mutant_name
///|```

fn mutant_name(m : BnMutant) -> String {
  match m {
    Correct => "correct  "
    NoEps => "no_eps   "
    NoMeanSub => "no_mean_sub"
    NoSqrt => "no_sqrt  "
    NoGamma => "no_gamma "
    NoBeta => "no_beta  "
    SwapAB => "swap_ab  "
    PerSample => "per_sample"
    Unbiased => "unbiased "
  }
}

///|
///|```text
///|mutant_forward : a self-contained BatchNorm1d with one deliberate flaw
///|```

///|
///|```text
///|mutant_forward
///|```

///| Written out in full rather than delegated to `batch_norm1d_forward`, which
///| is the point: a mutant has to be a DIFFERENT function from the one the
///| gates bless, otherwise the sweep is measuring the real code.
///|
fn mutant_forward(m : BnMutant, input : Array[Float]) -> Array[Float] {
  let n = BN1D_N
  let c = BN1D_C
  let w = BN1D_W
  let cw = c * w
  let out : Array[Float] = Array::make(n * cw, 0.0F)
  for ni in 0..<n {
    for ci in 0..<c {
      // PerSample reduces over the time axis only; every other variant
      // reduces over the whole batch.
      let per_sample = same_mutant(m, PerSample)
      let lo = if per_sample { ni } else { 0 }
      let hi = if per_sample { ni + 1 } else { n }
      let cnt_f = Float::from_int((hi - lo) * w)
      let mut sum = 0.0F
      for k in lo..<hi {
        for wi in 0..<w {
          sum = sum + input[k * cw + ci * w + wi]
        }
      }
      let mean = sum / cnt_f
      // Two-pass, matching production: E[(x - mean)^2]. `NoMeanSub` is the
      // one-pass E[x^2], which is the formula production used to compute and
      // the defect G1 caught.
      let mut sq_dev = 0.0F
      let mut sq_raw = 0.0F
      for k in lo..<hi {
        for wi in 0..<w {
          let v = input[k * cw + ci * w + wi]
          let d = v - mean
          sq_dev = sq_dev + d * d
          sq_raw = sq_raw + v * v
        }
      }
      let mut variance = if same_mutant(m, NoMeanSub) {
        sq_raw / cnt_f
      } else {
        sq_dev / cnt_f
      }
      // Unbiased variance: divisor (N*W - 1) instead of N*W.
      if same_mutant(m, Unbiased) {
        variance = variance * (cnt_f / (cnt_f - 1.0F))
      }
      let eps_used = if same_mutant(m, NoEps) { 0.0F } else { BN1D_EPS }
      let inv = if same_mutant(m, NoSqrt) {
        1.0F
      } else {
        1.0F / @core.sqrtf(variance + eps_used)
      }
      let mut g = bn1d_gamma[ci]
      let mut b = bn1d_beta[ci]
      if same_mutant(m, NoGamma) {
        g = 1.0F
      }
      if same_mutant(m, NoBeta) {
        b = 0.0F
      }
      if same_mutant(m, SwapAB) {
        let tmp = g
        g = bn1d_beta[ci]
        b = tmp
      }
      for wi in 0..<w {
        let v = input[ni * cw + ci * w + wi]
        out[ni * cw + ci * w + wi] = g * (v - mean) * inv + b
      }
    }
  }
  out
}

///|
///|```text
///|central_difference : d(loss)/dx for whichever forward is under test
///|```

///|
///|```text
///|central_difference
///|```

///| Differentiates the mutant's OWN forward. That is what makes the comparison
///| fair: the question is not "is this gradient wrong" but "would a
///| finite-difference gate have noticed", and a finite-difference gate always
///| differentiates the implementation it is testing. Against a PyTorch golden
///| the same mutant is trivially caught, and the sweep reports both columns so
///| the difference between the two kinds of gate is visible rather than
///| asserted.
///|
fn central_difference(m : BnMutant, h : Float) -> Array[Float] {
  let total = BN1D_N * BN1D_C * BN1D_W
  let out : Array[Float] = Array::make(total, 0.0F)
  let base = bn1d_input.copy()
  for i in 0..<total {
    let up = base.copy()
    let dn = base.copy()
    up[i] = up[i] + h
    dn[i] = dn[i] - h
    let lp = mutant_loss(m, up)
    let lm = mutant_loss(m, dn)
    out[i] = (lp - lm) / (2.0F * h)
  }
  out
}

///|
///|```text
///|mutant_loss
///|```

///|
///|```text
///|mutant_loss
///|```

///| The same scalar the PyTorch gradient was taken against: `SUM_n w_n *
///| SUM_{c,w} y[n,c,w]^2`.
///|
fn mutant_loss(m : BnMutant, input : Array[Float]) -> Float {
  let y = mutant_forward(m, input)
  let n = BN1D_N
  let cw = BN1D_C * BN1D_W
  let mut s = 0.0F
  for ni in 0..<n {
    let wt = bn1d_loss_weights[ni]
    for k in 0..<cw {
      s = s + wt * y[ni * cw + k] * y[ni * cw + k]
    }
  }
  s
}

///|
///|```text
///|G3: the golden catches what a finite-difference gate provably cannot
///|```

///|
///|```text
///|G3 mutation sweep
///|```

///| Runs every mutant through both kinds of gate and asserts three things:
///
///|   1. the correct implementation passes both;
///|   2. EVERY wrong implementation is caught by the PyTorch golden;
///|   3. at least one is missed by the finite-difference column.
///
///| The third assertion is the one worth keeping. It is what fails if someone
///| replaces the generated goldens with central differences "to keep the test
///| hermetic" -- an edit that looks like a simplification and silently removes
///| the only thing these gates can see that a self-consistency check cannot.
///|
test "G3 / golden catches every mutant; finite differences provably miss some" {
  let mutants = [
    Correct,
    NoEps,
    NoMeanSub,
    NoSqrt,
    NoGamma,
    NoBeta,
    SwapAB,
    PerSample,
    Unbiased,
  ]
  let mut missed_by_fd = 0
  let mut detail = ""
  for m in mutants {
    let y = mutant_forward(m, bn1d_input.copy())
    let (over_golden, worst_golden) = count_over(y, bn1d_y_golden, BN1D_FWD_ATOL, BN1D_TOL_FWD)

    // The gradient of THIS mutant's loss, taken two ways: the same central
    // difference a naive gate would use, and the PyTorch autograd golden.
    let fd = central_difference(m, 1.0e-2F)
    let (over_fd, _) = count_over(fd, bn1d_dx_golden, 0.0F, BN1D_FD_TOL)

    let is_correct = same_mutant(m, Correct)
    if is_correct {
      if over_golden > 0 || over_fd > 0 {
        fail(
          "G3: the correct implementation FAILS a gate -- golden \{over_golden}, " +
          "fd \{over_fd}. One of the two gates is wrong, not the code.",
        )
      }
    } else {
      if over_golden == 0 {
        fail(
          "G3: mutant \{mutant_name(m)} matched the PyTorch golden on all " +
          "\{bn1d_y_golden.length()} coordinates, worst relative " +
          "\{worst_golden}. The golden gate is too loose to see this mistake.",
        )
      }
      if over_fd == 0 {
        missed_by_fd = missed_by_fd + 1
        detail = detail + "\n  " + mutant_name(m) +
          ": golden catches it, finite differences do not"
      }
    }
  }
  if missed_by_fd == 0 {
    fail(
      "G3: a finite-difference check caught every mutant here. That is " +
      "suspicious rather than reassuring -- it means this fixture no longer " +
      "demonstrates why the PyTorch goldens are needed, and the assertion " +
      "that protects them has stopped testing anything." +
      detail,
    )
  }
}

///|
///|```text
///|G3a: the sweep is not vacuous -- Correct really does pass
///|```

///| Guard against the sweep degenerating: if `Correct` ever diverged, every
///| mutant would "fail" and `missed_by_fd` would stay 0, so G3 would fail for
///| the wrong reason and point at the fixture instead of at the real defect.
///|
test "G3a / correct implementation reproduces the PyTorch golden exactly" {
  let y = mutant_forward(Correct, bn1d_input.copy())
  let (over, worst) = count_over(y, bn1d_y_golden, BN1D_FWD_ATOL, BN1D_TOL_FWD)
  if over > 0 {
    fail("G3a: Correct diverges from the golden on \{over} coordinates, worst \{worst}")
  }
  // The production path and the mutant path must agree, or the sweep is
  // measuring a different function from the one the gates bless.
  let bn = bn1d_fixture()
  let (y_prod, _) = @nn.batch_norm1d_forward(
    bn1d_input.copy(),
    BN1D_N,
    BN1D_C,
    BN1D_W,
    bn,
  )
  let (over2, worst2) = count_over(y_prod, y, BN1D_FWD_ATOL, BN1D_TOL_FWD)
  if over2 > 0 {
    fail(
      "G3a: production and mutant 'Correct' disagree on \{over2} coordinates, " +
      "worst \{worst2}. The sweep is measuring a different function.",
    )
  }
}

///|
///|```text
///|batch_norm1d_size : the [n,c,w] element count
///|```

///|
///|```text
///|batch_norm1d_size
///|```

///| `batch_norm2d_forward` does not validate `input.length()`, so an over-long
///| buffer is read over its first `n*c*w` entries and looks entirely plausible.
///| The ASP context path in this project already produced one stride defect of
///| exactly that family, which is why `batch_norm1d_forward` aborts on a shape
///| mismatch rather than trusting the caller. This pins the arithmetic the
///| guard compares against; the guard itself is an `abort`, so it is exercised
///| by the shape tests in wave1's `batch_norm2d_test.mbt` rather than here.
///|
test "BatchNorm1d / size helper matches the fixture shape" {
  let want = @nn.batch_norm1d_size(BN1D_N, BN1D_C, BN1D_W)
  if want != bn1d_input.length() {
    fail(
      "batch_norm1d_size(\{BN1D_N},\{BN1D_C},\{BN1D_W}) = \{want}, but the " +
      "fixture holds \{bn1d_input.length()} elements",
    )
  }
  if @nn.batch_norm1d_size(4, 6, 7) != 168 {
    fail("batch_norm1d_size(4,6,7) should be 168")
  }
}
